1.The Fibonacci numbers are calculated by the following relation: Fn=Fn-1+Fn-2, With F1= F2 =1.

(1) Compute the first 50 Fibonacci numbers; store the results in a vector (use R-command “vector” to create it). You have to use a loop here.

(2) For large n, the ratio Fn/Fn-1 approaches the “golden mean”: .

(3) What is the value of F50/F49; is it equal to the golden mean?
(4) When is n large enough? (i.e. sufficiently close (<1×e-6) to the golden mean)

2. Create a script file which draws a curve of the function y=x3 sin2(3π x) in the interval [-2, 2].

Make a curve of the function y=1/cos(1+x2) in the interval [-5, 5].

3. Load in the stackloss dataset from within R

(1) Save the variables Water.Temp and Acid.Conc. in a data frame called tempacid.

(2). Compute the mean, variance, and 5 number summary of the variable stack.loss.

(3) Create a histogram, boxplot, and normal probability plot for the variable stack.loss. Does an assumption of normality seem appropriate for this sample?

4. (1) Simulate 20 observations from the binomial distribution with n=15 and p=0.2.

(2) Plot the Poisson mass function with λ = 4 over the range x = 0, 1, … 15.

(3) Simulate 100 observations from the normal distribution with μ = 50 and σ = 4. Plot the empirical cdf Fn(x) for this sample and overlay the true CDF F(x).

(4). Simulate 25 flips of a fair coin where the results are “heads” and “tails” (hint: use the sample() funct

5. Data airquality contains measurements of ozone concentration in New York from May to September 1983, together with other relevant variables.

(1) Plot the ozone concentration over time. Look at how different plotting types ("l", "h") affect the appearance. The EPA standard for ozone until recently was 120 ppb, 140 ppb was moderate nonattainment and 160 ppb serious nonattainment. Indicate these with horizontal lines in appropriate colors. Use the text() function to annotate the severe ozone days with their dates.

(2) Ozone is produced by chemical reactions in the air that require sunlight. Draw a scatterplot of ozone and solar radiation. Is the relationship monotone?

(3) Perhaps wind or temperature is responsible for the shape of the plot. Use the coplot function to draw scatterplots of ozone and solar radiation for different levels of wind speed, of temperature, and of both at once.

6. A famous data set that is part of R is the “iris” data set (Fisher, 1936), which gives measurements, in centimeters for sepal length and width and petal length and width, respectively, for 50 flowers of the species Iris setosa, Iris versicolor and Iris virginica. The flowers are typically different shades of blue or purple and the plants are different sizes, but as with many plants there is enough color and size variation that these are not sufficient to classify the species. I. setosa is notable in that the ’standards’, the vertical sepals in an iris flower, are almost non-existent, while I. virginica has the classic iris form.

(1) What is the class of the data set? why?

(2) What are the dimensions of the data set? (number of rows, columns)

(3) Produce a scatter plot of petal length against petal width; produce an informative title and labels of the two axes.

(4) Draw a histogram of sepal length. Are there visible subgroups?

(5) Plot sepal length and petal length for the irises, with different point colors for each species. This helps distinguish small flowers from large flowers with small sepals

(6) Do the same for petal length and width, and for sepal length and width.

(7) Now produce a similar box-and whisker plot for all four morphological measurements, arranged in two rows and two columns. First specify the graphical parameter that arranges the plots two by two.

7. The saturated oxygen concentration in water (μmol kg-1), as function of temperature (T), and salinity (S) can be calculated by: SatOx = exp(A)

where : A= -173.9894 + 25559.07/T + 146.4813×loge(T/100) -22.204×T/100 + S×(-0.037362+0.016504×T/100-0.0020564×T/100×T/100) and T is temperature in Kelvin (T kelvin = T celsius+273.15).

(1) Make a function that implements this formula; the default values for temperature and salinity are 20°C and 35 respectively.

(2) What is the saturated oxygen concentration at the default conditions? (A: 225.2346)
(3) Estimate the saturated oxygen concentration for a range of temperatures from 0 to 30°C, and salinity 35. (Tip: no need to use loops).

